---
date added: 2026-10-12
title: One-Pot French Onion Rigatoni
author: Toni Chapman
source: https://cooking.nytimes.com/recipes/769539003-one-pot-french-onion-rigatoni
tags: pasta, dinner, one pot, vegetarian
servings: 6
time required: 1 hour 15 minutes
cook time: 1 hour 10 minutes
notes: Yellow onions are ideal here — they caramelize evenly and develop deep sweetness without becoming cloying. For a heavy cream substitute, use half-and-half or even whole milk. Mozzarella can stand in for Gruyère.
---

Caramelize the onions: In a large #Dutch oven{} or wide, heavy-bottomed pot, heat the @unsalted butter{2%tablespoons} and @olive oil{1%tablespoon} over medium-low. Add the @large yellow onions{2} (thinly sliced) and cook, stirring every few minutes, until deeply browned, soft and jammy, about ~{45%minutes}.

Once the onions are caramelized, add @kosher salt{3/4%teaspoon}, several grinds of @freshly ground black pepper{}, the @garlic cloves{2} (minced), @fresh thyme leaves{1%teaspoon} and @Worcestershire sauce{2%teaspoons} and cook until fragrant, about ~{30%seconds}.

Deglaze the pot: Pour in the @dry white wine{1/4%cup} and cook, scraping up any browned bits from the bottom of the pot, until mostly reduced, about ~{30%seconds}.

Cook the pasta: Add the @mezzi rigatoni or regular rigatoni{1%pound} to the pot, then pour in enough @broth{5-6%cups} (beef recommended for deeper flavor, chicken for a lighter profile; vegetable broth also works) to just cover the pasta. Bring to a boil, then reduce the heat to medium and cook uncovered, stirring every 1 to 2 minutes, until the pasta is just tender and the liquid has thickened into a sauce, ~{15-18%minutes} depending on the brand and thickness of the pasta. If the liquid reduces too quickly (before the pasta is tender), add more broth, if you didn’t use it all, a splash at a time.

Finish the pasta: Lower the heat and stir in the @heavy cream{1/2%cup}. Remove the pot from the heat. Add the @shredded Gruyère cheese{1%cup} (about 5 ounces) a small handful at a time, stirring until fully melted and smooth before adding more. Once the Gruyère is fully incorporated, stir in the @finely grated Parmesan cheese{1/2%cup} (about 2 ounces) until the sauce is glossy and coats the pasta evenly. Taste and adjust seasoning with additional salt and pepper if needed.

Let rest for ~{5-7%minutes} before serving to allow the sauce to tighten. Serve warm, with extra Parmesan if desired.
